I'm standing in the booth and it's tight  in here because it's only made for one  but we've squished four of us in I have  my brother on one side and my twin  sister on the other and my mom is  standing behind me I'm too short to see  what she's doing and I'm only four years  old anyway so I'm a little bit antsy so  I started ducking down beneath the  curtain to check out the feet and listen  to the voices and I ducked down again  and I popped back up and I ducked down  for a third time and then there's a  bright flash play cool that had nothing  to do with me but sure enough when we  walk out of the booth someone asks my  mom for my name and information I have  been a political junkie for as long as I  can remember or at least since my star  turn in the local paper back in nineteen  ninety i was born and raised in New  Hampshire home of the first in the  nation presidential primary and by  middle school I was writing letters to  the editor on the issues of the day in  high school I was running for everything  Student Senate which I won homecoming  queen which I lost but I was no  shrinking violet and I fast forward four  years later and it's my senior year and  I'm at Bowdoin College in Brunswick  Maine it's my spring semester and I'm in  this awesome class 10 students 5vs5 all  geared towards running a campus-wide  election and my team knows my history  and they look at me and they say Val you  have to run you have to be our candidate  you would be awesome and in that moment  I froze I was so afraid of putting  myself out there of being a bad  candidate of losing and so  I said no it's not for me I'm more of a  behind-the-scenes type of person I guess  that was something I'd internalized over  the last four years you do it tomm you  would be awesome you'd be our candidate  and he was awesome he ran and he won and  I fast forward another month later and  I'm sitting in the area living room of  my college presidents house I'm there  for a meeting with his wife asking for  help to get down to Washington DC to  work for a female senator she's a  serious person she went on subsequently  to be a cabinet secretary under  President Obama and I'm staring at this  oil painting in the corner of the room  not because I care about it but because  she's just asked me a question Val would  you ever consider running for public  office no I say it's not for me I'm more  of a behind-the-scenes type of person  like a chief of staff and she says how  can you say that you care so deeply  about women being elected to office if  not you who I've been turning that  question over in my mind for some time  and i'm here today to challenge the  paradigm that women like me and women  like all of you are more  behind-the-scenes type of people not  candidate material and to the man in the  audience don't worry this is important  for you to because we're all better  served when more women run for office  and we have more diverse representation  so what's the problem what's the big  deal well in 2012 Congress held a  hearing on the very thorny issue of  reproductive rights and contraceptives  and to debate that issue they bought  they brought in a panel of experts to  testify before the committee  and I don't care where you fall on this  spectrum this cannot be the best way to  talk about this issue we have to do  better but let's level set where are we  we know that women make up roughly forty  seven percent of the workforce and we  know that in recent years they've  received roughly fifty seven percent of  college degrees but when we look across  the country women make up just twelve  percent of Governors offices and less  than twenty percent of Congress that  twenty percent by the way is an all-time  high and it ties us with an unlikely  friend and ally turkmenistan so how did  we get here we know that when women run  they are as likely to win as their male  counterparts and yet a 2004 brown study  found that men were seventy percent more  likely to indicate a desire to run for  office and I suppose that's not all that  surprising when you consider another  study that said that a woman has to be  asked seven times before she considers  running for office most men need to be  encouraged just one time and that  encouragement can come from the face  staring right back at them in the mirror  I get it it's hard to run for office  you're putting yourself out there you're  making yourself vulnerable it's messy  and there's mudslinging and truthfully I  talked to a US Congresswoman recently  who told me that she got nauseous every  day on her first campaign just thinking  about going out there and getting  herself out in front of voters but you  are the women of the GSB you've never  shied away from doing something hard if  not you who so I came to the GSB and I  had dozens of conversations with my male  and female classmates and i said i'm  going to take it local I've had  conversations many of you in this room I  think and I asked would you ever  consider running for office and more  often than not our male classmates said  yeah I'm definitely considering it 100%  or I haven't considered it but I would  be great and on occasion hell yes I  should be President what is the holdup  and more often they're not our female  classmates on the other hand responded  with it's not for me I'm more of a  behind-the-scenes type of person and so  I dug a little bit deeper and I did a  survey of close to 300 of our classmates  and i asked the same question would you  ever consider running for anything and  the response was all too familiar of our  male classmates that responded  seventy-seven percent all of you said  yes i will run for office someday I hope  to the women just forty four percent so  why does it matter these issues that are  being discussed in the political arena  are incredibly important we're talking  about health care education the economy  these are decisions that are going to  impact our lives and our children's  lives and our grandchildren's lives and  you know what when women run they are  really good at what they do because  studies have shown that they are two  times as effective compared to their  male counterparts getting a bill out of  committee and getting that bill enacted  but when women don't run so many voices  go unheard so what can we do we can run  there are tons of resources out there to  help you be successful dozens in  California alone and I'm standing up  here offering to be a resource for you  as well I spent close to five years  working in Congress before I came to the  GSB I've been up close and personal I  promise you are all qualified to serve  in Congress trust me and I'm here taking  my own advice because now I can answer  yes I am considering a run for office I  hope I will have the opportunity to  serve at some point in the future I've  come a long way from that college senior  I challenge all of you to do the same  and today I want you to take a  meaningful step in that direction if  you've counted yourself out ask yourself  why or why not you if you've never  considered it consider it and if you're  thinking about it at all let's talk I  have a really long history with  elections to everybody in the room  including the men if you've thought long  and hard about running for office and  ultimately decided it's not for you I  hope you will identify encourage support  and work for a really good female  candidate you are the students of the  GSB you are the best and the brightest  the most highly educated the most  well-connected you are the future of  this country if not you who thank you
 

